{"id":"h192","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"science-physics","question":"A nonconducting spherical shell, with an inner radius of $4.0 \\mathrm{~cm}$ and an outer radius of $6.0 \\mathrm{~cm}$, has charge spread nonuniformly through its volume between its inner and outer surfaces. The volume charge density $\\rho$ is the charge per unit volume, with the unit coulomb per cubic meter. For this shell $\\rho=b / r$, where $r$ is the distance in meters from the center of the shell and $b=3.0 \\mu \\mathrm{C} / \\mathrm{m}^2$. What is the net charge in the shell?\nIs the correct answer \"$3.8$ $10^{-8} \\mathrm{C}$\"?","truth":"yes","model_answer":"yes","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"The density ρ = b/r with b in μC/m² and r in meters gives units of C/m³, so the units are consistent.","confidence":0.9,"lean":1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The charge should be found by integrating ρ over thin spherical shells with volume element 4πr² dr.","confidence":0.97,"lean":1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The integral reduces to Q = 2πb(R2² − R1²).","confidence":0.97,"lean":1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":0.0},{"text":"R2² − R1² equals 0.0020 m² when the radii are converted to meters.","confidence":0.95,"lean":1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Evaluating gives Q ≈ 3.77×10^-8 C, which rounds to 3.8×10^-8 C.","confidence":0.96,"lean":1,"theta":1.508,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The r² in the volume element cancels the 1/r in the density, leaving a simple integral.","confidence":0.9,"lean":1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Possible pitfalls such as wrong radii, cm/m mixups, or the wrong volume element are avoided in this calculation.","confidence":0.9,"lean":1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.0},{"text":"There are no considerations of substance against the proposed answer.","confidence":0.9,"lean":1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The proposed answer of 3.8×10^-8 C is correct.","confidence":0.96,"lean":1,"theta":1.508,"phi":0.0}],"relations